WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.007 --> 00:00:09.175
Planes, trains

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:12.846
and automobiles.

00:00:12.929 --> 00:00:14.681
Sure, these marvels of modern technology

00:00:14.681 --> 00:00:16.891
can get you somewhere fast.

00:00:17.684 --> 00:00:20.103
But are they worth
all the hassle and risk?

00:00:21.062 --> 00:00:24.065
No. You deserve better.

00:00:24.065 --> 00:00:27.193
You deserve an airship.

00:00:30.321 --> 00:00:32.407
And not just any airship.

00:00:33.199 --> 00:00:34.743
I'm offering you a ride

00:00:34.743 --> 00:00:37.454
in Google co-founder
Sergey Brin’s airship

00:00:38.079 --> 00:00:39.497
made by the good people

00:00:39.497 --> 00:00:41.332
at Lighter Than Air.

00:00:41.332 --> 00:00:43.001
That's right, friends.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:45.462
Airships are back again

00:00:45.462 --> 00:00:48.423
and they're better than ever.

00:00:48.757 --> 00:00:50.341
They're less slow.

00:00:50.341 --> 00:00:52.052
They're less noisy.

00:00:52.052 --> 00:00:54.554
They have windows that open.

00:00:54.554 --> 00:00:57.098
That's crazy.

00:00:57.307 --> 00:00:59.559
And now they're less
flammable, too.

00:01:00.435 --> 00:01:02.979
So come on down to my hometown
of Mountain View, California

00:01:02.979 --> 00:01:07.442
and hitch your ride
on the Pathfinder One.

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:19.454
About five years ago,
I got wind

00:01:19.662 --> 00:01:22.457
that Sergey Brin

00:01:22.457 --> 00:01:27.128
was funding the construction
of a secret airship right here

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:29.923
at NASA Ames in Mountain View.

00:01:30.965 --> 00:01:31.424
I saw some

00:01:31.424 --> 00:01:33.510
pictures of this early
airship.

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:35.345
Got kind of obsessed with it.

00:01:35.345 --> 00:01:37.472
Sergey didn't want to talk
about his airship.

00:01:37.889 --> 00:01:41.684
But over the last couple of years,
I managed to ingratiate myself

00:01:41.893 --> 00:01:44.896
with the guy who's bringing
Sergey Brin’s airship to life.

00:01:45.688 --> 00:01:46.981
And today

00:01:46.981 --> 00:01:50.276
I'm going to take you guys in
to see this thing

00:01:50.276 --> 00:01:51.903
being made.

00:02:00.036 --> 00:02:01.329
I'm Alan Weston,

00:02:01.329 --> 00:02:04.082
and I'm the CEO of LTA,

00:02:04.332 --> 00:02:08.086
and I'm the first person
to work on this project.

00:02:08.628 --> 00:02:10.755
We've got this beauty
behind us.

00:02:10.755 --> 00:02:12.882
Give us, give us the basics.

00:02:12.882 --> 00:02:14.676
This is Pathfinder one.

00:02:14.676 --> 00:02:17.011
This is 122 meters long,

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:19.639
has about 28
tons of total lift.

00:02:20.056 --> 00:02:21.975
It's designed to cruise

00:02:21.975 --> 00:02:23.977
at maximum speed of 60 knots.

00:02:24.644 --> 00:02:27.188
It could probably go over 2,000

00:02:27.188 --> 00:02:29.315
nautical miles in one leg.

00:02:30.108 --> 00:02:32.986
Our mission is to expand

00:02:32.986 --> 00:02:36.322
and complement
existing humanitarian relief

00:02:36.739 --> 00:02:38.825
using lighter
than air technology

00:02:39.367 --> 00:02:42.245
and at the same time
reduce the carbon footprint

00:02:42.245 --> 00:02:43.079
of aviation.

00:02:44.122 --> 00:02:46.791
Al here might look like
your average aviation nerd,

00:02:47.292 --> 00:02:49.127
but I assure you he's not.

00:02:53.798 --> 00:02:55.425
Among other claims to fame,

00:02:55.425 --> 00:02:57.594
he was a member
of the Dangerous Sports Club,

00:02:58.803 --> 00:02:59.804
where he would do stuff

00:02:59.804 --> 00:03:01.055
like this

00:03:02.599 --> 00:03:03.933
and this.

00:03:05.185 --> 00:03:06.186
He even performed

00:03:06.186 --> 00:03:08.646
one of the first modern bungee
jumps.

00:03:08.646 --> 00:03:11.649
People had sort of used vines
and things like that before,

00:03:11.649 --> 00:03:12.817
but you guys were
the first ones

00:03:12.817 --> 00:03:15.320
to get just a cord
and jump off a bridge, right?

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:17.197
Right.
It was an April Fools’ joke.

00:03:17.197 --> 00:03:18.781
And so, yes.

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:23.661
And they let somebody like
you go work

00:03:23.661 --> 00:03:25.830
for the Air Force after that.

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:28.833
Oh, actually, the Air Force
thought it was pretty funny.

00:03:37.884 --> 00:03:39.260
Much like Al, airships

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:41.679
have a fascinating backstory.

00:03:41.679 --> 00:03:43.848
Some of the first ones
the US ever made

00:03:43.848 --> 00:03:45.433
lived right here in this hangar.

00:03:49.771 --> 00:03:51.481
Almost 100 years ago,

00:03:51.481 --> 00:03:53.858
the Navy began
building airships in Ohio

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:57.278
so they could be flown
to California.

00:03:57.278 --> 00:03:59.030
The Navy saw these craft as key

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:02.075
to gathering intelligence
and performing reconnaissance.

00:04:02.742 --> 00:04:05.286
It was the glory days
of airships.

00:04:05.286 --> 00:04:07.121
You’ve shown me
some of these slides

00:04:07.121 --> 00:04:08.373
just here in Silicon Valley.

00:04:08.373 --> 00:04:09.666
There were times
when the sky was,

00:04:09.666 --> 00:04:12.669
like, filled with airships
and they were doing

00:04:12.669 --> 00:04:13.294
amazing things.

00:04:13.294 --> 00:04:15.922
I mean, there’s one that was
almost like an aircraft carrier.

00:04:16.339 --> 00:04:18.841
This was actually in 1932
and ‘33.

00:04:19.384 --> 00:04:20.885
There was a hook on
the airship

00:04:20.885 --> 00:04:24.013
and biplanes were carried
inside the airship.

00:04:24.013 --> 00:04:26.808
And they were refueled.

00:04:28.851 --> 00:04:30.395
Then of course, airships

00:04:30.395 --> 00:04:32.480
had some very bad PR.

00:04:33.439 --> 00:04:35.817
And planes came along,

00:04:35.817 --> 00:04:37.193
and people lost interest

00:04:37.193 --> 00:04:39.279
in these majestic beasts.

00:04:39.279 --> 00:04:42.907
Large airships
disappeared in 1939.

00:04:43.366 --> 00:04:44.075
So like everything

00:04:44.075 --> 00:04:46.703
people might think of,
those are all blimps.

00:04:46.703 --> 00:04:47.870
And the difference being,
a blimp

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:49.956
just has no rigid structure.

00:04:49.956 --> 00:04:50.498
Right.

00:04:50.665 --> 00:04:52.542
This is a rigid airship
with a skeleton.

00:04:52.542 --> 00:04:55.295
And so the skeleton defines
the shape.

00:04:55.295 --> 00:04:56.587
And Al thinks he can bring

00:04:56.587 --> 00:04:58.923
these sexy, hard-bodied
airships back,

00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:01.301
this time filled with nice,

00:05:01.301 --> 00:05:03.428
non-flammable helium.

00:05:03.428 --> 00:05:05.596
So you've got this
wonderful history

00:05:05.847 --> 00:05:08.141
and you're, like,
making it all happen again.

00:05:08.308 --> 00:05:08.975
Right, right.

00:05:08.975 --> 00:05:10.685
Back to the future. Yeah.

00:05:10.685 --> 00:05:13.730
We’re restarting, we’re awakening
the sleeping giants

00:05:13.730 --> 00:05:15.940
is what I like to say.
It’s one of my favorite phrases.

00:05:21.029 --> 00:05:22.613
Can we do the horn?

00:05:22.613 --> 00:05:23.573
Sure.

00:05:26.951 --> 00:05:30.913
I don't think anyone I've ever
heard anyone use it before.

00:05:33.958 --> 00:05:35.626
This is Carl Taussig.

00:05:35.626 --> 00:05:38.046
He's a mechanical engineer
I've known for years

00:05:38.046 --> 00:05:40.548
who really gets around
Silicon Valley.

00:05:42.467 --> 00:05:45.011
I asked him to give me
the grand tour.

00:05:45.928 --> 00:05:47.472
It's a lot of scaffolding.

00:05:47.472 --> 00:05:50.683
Yeah, it's incredible.

00:05:50.683 --> 00:05:51.434
At first glance,

00:05:51.434 --> 00:05:54.228
it appears almost nothing has
changed in a hundred years.

00:05:54.729 --> 00:05:57.398
But really, this thing
is a mix of the super old

00:05:57.398 --> 00:05:59.317
and the super new.

00:05:59.317 --> 00:06:01.486
So this is one of 12

00:06:01.486 --> 00:06:03.821
of the electric motors
that propel the ship.

00:06:03.821 --> 00:06:04.447
Okay.

00:06:04.447 --> 00:06:06.574
And it's you can see it's

00:06:06.574 --> 00:06:09.535
motors mounted on a pylon
which can rotate.

00:06:09.535 --> 00:06:11.996
So think of this airship
as a giant quadcopter.

00:06:12.372 --> 00:06:13.956
All these are thrust vectored.

00:06:13.956 --> 00:06:16.292
They can be pointed
in order to lift the ship

00:06:16.292 --> 00:06:19.212
straight up or move laterally.

00:06:19.337 --> 00:06:21.672
And then here,
this outer material is,

00:06:21.672 --> 00:06:24.592
it’s kind of leveraged
from racing sailboats.

00:06:25.218 --> 00:06:26.469
This is how it's laced on,

00:06:26.469 --> 00:06:29.138
and this is a kind of
an anachronism from

00:06:29.138 --> 00:06:31.391
the way airships were built
100 years ago.

00:06:32.058 --> 00:06:34.936
There's
40,000 of these lacing tabs.

00:06:35.353 --> 00:06:36.729
Each one is laced by hand.

00:06:36.729 --> 00:06:37.146
Jeez.

00:06:43.444 --> 00:06:46.864
The Pathfinder One’s skeleton is
made of carbon fiber, making it

00:06:46.864 --> 00:06:49.200
lighter and stronger than past
airships.

00:06:50.243 --> 00:06:52.161
The ship will run on diesel
to start,

00:06:52.161 --> 00:06:54.580
but LTA hopes to switch
to either solar

00:06:54.664 --> 00:06:58.209
or some form of hydrogen power
in the future.

00:06:58.418 --> 00:07:00.920
Overall, the airship will be
much slower than a plane,

00:07:01.212 --> 00:07:03.423
but at least the passengers
will feel virtuous

00:07:03.589 --> 00:07:05.800
about their carbon footprint.

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:08.678
I've never been in a gondola.

00:07:10.346 --> 00:07:12.932
Designed to seat about ten people.

00:07:12.932 --> 00:07:15.643
It's used commercially
for sightseeing

00:07:15.643 --> 00:07:18.438
by the Zeppelin company
on their NT airships.

00:07:18.646 --> 00:07:21.649
And so if you're doing like a

00:07:21.649 --> 00:07:22.567
two or three day

00:07:22.567 --> 00:07:25.319
journey from London
to New York, you're just

00:07:25.319 --> 00:07:27.613
you're hanging out in here
in this space.

00:07:28.531 --> 00:07:30.575
Admittedly,
for a futuristic airship,

00:07:30.658 --> 00:07:33.119
this is an underwhelming
interior.

00:07:33.119 --> 00:07:35.413
But this ship is
just an experimental vehicle,

00:07:35.913 --> 00:07:38.207
the first of its kind,
and not for the public.

00:07:38.833 --> 00:07:40.793
And the company
has bigger things in mind

00:07:40.793 --> 00:07:43.087
than just
leisurely sky cruises.

00:07:43.087 --> 00:07:44.672
I think you guys are most

00:07:44.672 --> 00:07:46.966
interested
in kind of disaster scenarios.

00:07:46.966 --> 00:07:49.010
I mean, that's the heart of
why you're making this thing.

00:07:49.135 --> 00:07:49.760
Right.

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:52.263
When you arrive at a disaster
site,

00:07:52.263 --> 00:07:55.558
you often show up
and the place is wrecked.

00:07:55.558 --> 00:07:57.685
And so
the port may not be working.

00:07:58.769 --> 00:08:01.856
You then have to get your supplies
to where the disaster is.

00:08:02.356 --> 00:08:04.734
And typically, you know,
the roads are gone.

00:08:05.818 --> 00:08:08.529
Helicopters are really
expensive to operate.

00:08:08.863 --> 00:08:10.156
They have limited range

00:08:10.156 --> 00:08:12.158
and limited capacity
to lift things.

00:08:12.450 --> 00:08:14.702
So that's where an airship can
also help.

00:08:14.702 --> 00:08:15.786
And it's the cargo, right?

00:08:15.786 --> 00:08:18.706
I mean, they can carry
a significant amount of stuff.

00:08:19.207 --> 00:08:19.582
Right.

00:08:19.582 --> 00:08:22.084
We've studied airships
that could carry up

00:08:22.084 --> 00:08:24.337
to 200 tons of cargo.

00:08:24.337 --> 00:08:27.965
Our approach is scalable
to build larger airships

00:08:27.965 --> 00:08:31.010
that can carry even more.

00:08:33.930 --> 00:08:38.518
All right, we've gone
tail to snout.

00:08:39.894 --> 00:08:41.896
So this giant thing

00:08:41.896 --> 00:08:44.649
is like a custom
made anchor, kind of.

00:08:44.982 --> 00:08:46.067
Exactly.

00:08:46.067 --> 00:08:48.444
This is what we call our
our mast trailer.

00:08:48.444 --> 00:08:49.445
Okay.

00:08:50.154 --> 00:08:52.240
It’s one of the major advantages
of an airship.

00:08:52.865 --> 00:08:55.493
All you need to land is
one of these portable masts.

00:08:55.993 --> 00:08:57.203
You know, we really

00:08:57.203 --> 00:08:58.955
we hope to be flying
by the end of the year.

00:08:58.955 --> 00:09:00.790
Yeah.
There's a lot to do, still.

00:09:00.790 --> 00:09:02.291
Al promised my two kids

00:09:02.291 --> 00:09:04.085
one of the first, first rides.

00:09:04.085 --> 00:09:05.169
It'll be a great ride.

00:09:06.754 --> 00:09:07.505
Your kids, the

00:09:07.505 --> 00:09:09.549
flight test engineers.

00:09:09.549 --> 00:09:10.758
Just keep them safe.

00:09:15.263 --> 00:09:16.764
And as much as I want my kids

00:09:16.764 --> 00:09:18.933
waving to me from the gondola
on that

00:09:18.933 --> 00:09:20.351
very first flight...

00:09:23.062 --> 00:09:25.273
I can't help
but imagine things going

00:09:25.273 --> 00:09:28.150
horribly,
disastrously wrong.

00:09:29.860 --> 00:09:30.820
It is unfortunate

00:09:30.820 --> 00:09:33.322
that airships are synonymous
with fiery death.

00:09:34.532 --> 00:09:35.324
But ever since that

00:09:35.324 --> 00:09:37.868
fateful day in 1937,

00:09:37.868 --> 00:09:39.870
one small town in Germany,

00:09:39.870 --> 00:09:41.622
the home of the Hindenburg,

00:09:41.622 --> 00:09:44.417
has led a PR campaign
to resuscitate

00:09:44.417 --> 00:09:46.419
the airship’s image.

00:09:56.804 --> 00:09:58.306
The dream of the zeppelin

00:09:58.306 --> 00:10:00.641
is alive in Friedrichshafen.

00:10:05.438 --> 00:10:07.982
The town is embedded
with airship propaganda.

00:10:08.649 --> 00:10:10.568
And they start ‘em young.

00:10:11.611 --> 00:10:13.613
This is your gaseous god.

00:10:15.406 --> 00:10:17.658
There’s a parallel world
where this is the norm.

00:10:18.492 --> 00:10:20.786
And I'll admit I'm jealous.

00:10:21.746 --> 00:10:23.623
As far as weird stories go,

00:10:23.623 --> 00:10:24.999
this one is wonderfully weird.

00:10:26.626 --> 00:10:28.628
Count Ferdinand von Zeppelin

00:10:28.628 --> 00:10:30.463
was a great airship pioneer

00:10:30.463 --> 00:10:33.257
in the late
1800s and early 1900s,

00:10:34.133 --> 00:10:36.302
and the man
could not be stopped.

00:10:37.261 --> 00:10:39.722
He convinced all of Germany
to fund his work

00:10:39.930 --> 00:10:42.183
in an early version
of a Kickstarter campaign,

00:10:42.933 --> 00:10:45.853
and his company even managed
to convince the Allies into

00:10:45.853 --> 00:10:47.855
letting Germany
keep making airships

00:10:47.855 --> 00:10:49.982
after World War One.

00:10:50.316 --> 00:10:52.735
The count died in 1917.

00:10:52.735 --> 00:10:53.653
Shame.

00:10:54.487 --> 00:10:55.863
But he donated

00:10:55.863 --> 00:10:57.948
all his zeppelin
intellectual property

00:10:58.074 --> 00:11:00.701
to the city
of Friedrichshafen.

00:11:00.701 --> 00:11:01.661
Wunderbar!

00:11:03.287 --> 00:11:04.538
And bizarrely,

00:11:04.538 --> 00:11:06.832
his inventions were picked up
by the aerospace

00:11:06.832 --> 00:11:10.044
and automotive industries
and turned into the basis

00:11:10.044 --> 00:11:12.046
of a multi-billion-dollar
empire.

00:11:13.297 --> 00:11:14.215
Friedrichshafen

00:11:14.215 --> 00:11:17.677
gets a big chunk of this money
today, and its residents

00:11:17.677 --> 00:11:21.013
get free vacations and free
education as a result.

00:11:21.639 --> 00:11:22.556
Well done, count.

00:11:23.641 --> 00:11:28.521
This museum is partly financed by the Zeppelin Foundation,

00:11:28.521 --> 00:11:31.315
but so is the university, for example.

00:11:31.315 --> 00:11:34.402
We also have kindergartens, swimming pools, etc.

00:11:34.402 --> 00:11:38.823
So there is quite a bit of prosperity here that can be 
traced back quite clearly

00:11:38.823 --> 00:11:41.951
to Graf Zeppelin and his entrepreneurial spirit.

00:11:43.369 --> 00:11:46.497
So he was really an innovative spirit,

00:11:46.497 --> 00:11:50.876
and he actually founded the first Silicon Valley in Friedrichshafen.

00:11:51.252 --> 00:11:52.962
Where does people's
fascination

00:11:52.962 --> 00:11:54.755
with zeppelins come from?

00:11:54.755 --> 00:11:55.756
You know, what, like,

00:11:55.756 --> 00:11:58.050
why are these things
so interesting?

00:11:58.050 --> 00:12:01.679
I think that has a lot to do with the Hindenburg, of course,

00:12:01.679 --> 00:12:02.888
and with the accident of the Hindenburg.

00:12:09.520 --> 00:12:11.105
Tragedy strikes. A terrific

00:12:11.105 --> 00:12:13.065
explosion in the tail
fin of the dirigible

00:12:13.065 --> 00:12:15.025
and passengers and crew
hurl themselves out of

00:12:15.025 --> 00:12:17.903
the ship and to the ground.

00:12:18.028 --> 00:12:21.031
Maybe if there wasn't video
and all this

00:12:21.031 --> 00:12:22.992
reporting about this event,

00:12:22.992 --> 00:12:25.828
maybe airships wouldn't
have fallen out of favor

00:12:25.828 --> 00:12:28.122
as much as they did
for a period.

00:12:28.122 --> 00:12:29.039
Right, exactly.

00:12:29.039 --> 00:12:32.209
And it was also, of course, during the time of National Socialism.

00:12:32.209 --> 00:12:35.629
And if you look at feature films today, for example,

00:12:35.629 --> 00:12:38.966
or look at video games where the Zeppelin also appears

00:12:38.966 --> 00:12:43.429
as an instrument of war or as a substitute for an airplane,

00:12:43.429 --> 00:12:47.349
it’s often Nazis who are in these airships.

00:12:48.100 --> 00:12:48.642
Despite their

00:12:48.642 --> 00:12:51.020
reputation as both fiery
death traps

00:12:51.270 --> 00:12:53.147
and Nazi tools of war...

00:12:57.359 --> 00:12:59.028
I still want to ride one.

00:13:02.114 --> 00:13:04.575
Thar she blows.

00:13:05.910 --> 00:13:08.412
My airship awaits.

00:13:08.496 --> 00:13:09.789
Everyone tells you how awesome

00:13:09.789 --> 00:13:11.040
the experience is going to be.

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:13.959
Now they've talked me into it.

00:13:42.655 --> 00:13:43.447
This is crazy.

00:13:43.447 --> 00:13:45.699
You can like,
you feel this incline,

00:13:45.699 --> 00:13:47.576
but so gently.

00:13:51.956 --> 00:13:54.750
Holy **** man, this is crazy.

00:13:58.295 --> 00:14:02.049
I genuinely feel like a wizard.
It’s like...

00:14:02.591 --> 00:14:04.802
It's so hard to explain.

00:14:04.802 --> 00:14:07.805
Like surfing in the sky.

00:14:13.644 --> 00:14:16.230
Of course, the real wizards
sit up front.

00:14:18.482 --> 00:14:22.528
Meet Katharine Board,
one of the zeppelin flying elite.

00:14:23.445 --> 00:14:25.239
It's got to be a relatively

00:14:25.239 --> 00:14:28.325
small club
out of all the pilot clubs.

00:14:28.325 --> 00:14:30.661
Maybe this is the smallest? Or...

00:14:30.661 --> 00:14:31.662
No- notice,

00:14:31.662 --> 00:14:33.998
whenever you mention a pilot's
name, we tend to know them.

00:14:34.665 --> 00:14:35.916
It's a very small club.

00:14:35.916 --> 00:14:38.794
There are more astronauts
than airship pilots.

00:14:38.794 --> 00:14:39.795
Okay. All right.

00:14:39.795 --> 00:14:42.464
And so that means what,
we're talking about, like...

00:14:43.007 --> 00:14:46.886
Uh, maybe
15 zeppelin pilots.

00:14:46.886 --> 00:14:47.469
Okay.

00:14:52.057 --> 00:14:52.725
The great

00:14:52.725 --> 00:14:55.394
thing about flying an airship
is it’s fly by feel,

00:14:55.644 --> 00:14:56.896
not by numbers.

00:14:59.481 --> 00:15:01.358
The big airplanes, it’s
very much fly by numbers.

00:15:01.358 --> 00:15:02.109
Yeah.

00:15:02.109 --> 00:15:04.236
Okay yeah, there is a little bit
of feel and skill

00:15:04.486 --> 00:15:05.487
when they finally actually do

00:15:05.487 --> 00:15:06.780
touch the controls,
which is almost never.

00:15:07.865 --> 00:15:09.575
But it's different every day.

00:15:09.575 --> 00:15:11.535
And we go on the road
and we fly out of fields.

00:15:11.535 --> 00:15:13.996
We fly out of big airports,
small airports.

00:15:15.956 --> 00:15:16.999
There's something like,
I don't know

00:15:16.999 --> 00:15:18.042
if magical is the right word.

00:15:18.042 --> 00:15:20.294
It’s almost like an
illusion in your head, though,

00:15:20.294 --> 00:15:22.588
to see this big thing
that kind of moves

00:15:22.588 --> 00:15:25.674
gracefully,
but slowly and it's just so

00:15:25.674 --> 00:15:27.301
unlike any other aircraft
you're used to.

00:15:27.301 --> 00:15:28.552
It is a ship.

00:15:32.348 --> 00:15:34.475
Just looking at
how the ship loading

00:15:34.475 --> 00:15:36.810
and the center of buoyancy,
center of gravity,

00:15:36.810 --> 00:15:39.688
center of lift, all these things
are exactly the same

00:15:39.688 --> 00:15:40.773
because we do float.

00:15:40.773 --> 00:15:42.274
We float in the air

00:15:42.274 --> 00:15:44.151
just like a boat on the water.
Only difference is

00:15:44.151 --> 00:15:45.527
you can't see the waves.

00:15:48.614 --> 00:15:50.240
I got to poke my head
out the window.

00:15:50.240 --> 00:15:51.325
Yeah do that, yeah.

00:15:54.161 --> 00:15:56.288
That's crazy.

00:16:00.918 --> 00:16:04.338
I feel like a dog.
Full happiness, everything.

00:16:05.673 --> 00:16:09.134
You know, I don't love heights,
but when I stick my head out

00:16:09.134 --> 00:16:11.553
the window, it's
not scary or anything

00:16:11.553 --> 00:16:12.304
at all.

00:16:13.764 --> 00:16:15.099
So cool.

00:16:18.102 --> 00:16:21.563
Now there are these startups
trying to build new airships.

00:16:21.855 --> 00:16:23.774
Is this like an exciting
time for you,

00:16:23.774 --> 00:16:26.527
The idea that that maybe
we have new airships and

00:16:26.860 --> 00:16:29.321
and this technology
is coming back again?

00:16:29.947 --> 00:16:35.202
It’s a great idea and of course it would be great
if that would work out, the American project.

00:16:35.577 --> 00:16:38.330
I’m curious to see if that can be achieved.

00:16:51.677 --> 00:16:54.346
To find out if
the airship has a future,

00:16:54.346 --> 00:16:56.265
I headed back to America.

00:16:56.765 --> 00:16:58.684
Did I take a zeppelin?

00:16:58.684 --> 00:17:00.269
Only in my dreams.

00:17:09.945 --> 00:17:11.697
If Lighter Than Air
is successful,

00:17:11.697 --> 00:17:14.074
then the future of the airship
will take place

00:17:14.074 --> 00:17:16.326
not in Germany or Silicon
Valley...

00:17:18.495 --> 00:17:21.707
But here, in Akron,
Ohio, where it all began

00:17:21.707 --> 00:17:23.709
in the first place.

00:17:24.376 --> 00:17:26.545
The Navy airships USS Akron

00:17:26.545 --> 00:17:29.590
and USS Macon were built
here in the 1930s.

00:17:30.215 --> 00:17:32.676
Goodyear kept going with this work
and created

00:17:32.676 --> 00:17:35.012
its blimping empire.

00:17:35.012 --> 00:17:37.598
Now Lighter Than Air
has taken over this facility

00:17:37.806 --> 00:17:39.767
known as the airdock. It's

00:17:39.767 --> 00:17:43.103
where Sergei's even bigger
airships will come to life.

00:17:43.645 --> 00:17:46.899
Thank you for joining us
here in the Akron Airdock.

00:17:46.899 --> 00:17:47.983
Behind me,

00:17:47.983 --> 00:17:50.652
you can see the beginning
of our next airship

00:17:50.652 --> 00:17:52.905
that we're building
right here in Akron.

00:17:52.905 --> 00:17:56.325
It will be the largest airship
built in the United States

00:17:56.700 --> 00:17:59.369
since the Macon
was built in 1932.

00:18:00.287 --> 00:18:01.455
This is our very first test

00:18:01.455 --> 00:18:04.208
section, and this is where
we're going to build

00:18:04.458 --> 00:18:07.086
Pathfinder 3,
which will be 600 feet long

00:18:07.586 --> 00:18:09.671
and a hundred feet in diameter

00:18:10.005 --> 00:18:12.132
with a total lift of nearly
100 tons.

00:18:12.633 --> 00:18:15.260
Why did you come
to this facility?

00:18:15.260 --> 00:18:17.596
Just for the history
and the people, but I guess

00:18:17.596 --> 00:18:20.682
just, and its size offers
you something else as well.

00:18:21.100 --> 00:18:23.185
Yes. Here we are building

00:18:23.185 --> 00:18:25.813
the airship factory
of the future.

00:18:25.813 --> 00:18:28.690
We can build
larger airships here

00:18:28.816 --> 00:18:31.735
year round, than you can build
anywhere else in the world.

00:18:32.236 --> 00:18:33.612
So that's why we're here.

00:18:36.657 --> 00:18:37.574
If you want to,

00:18:37.574 --> 00:18:39.368
we can probably organize
someone to take you

00:18:39.368 --> 00:18:40.577
to the top of the airdock.

00:18:40.577 --> 00:18:41.829
What is it? An elevator?

00:18:41.829 --> 00:18:42.663
Or do I have to climb?

00:18:42.663 --> 00:18:43.580
No no, there’s a funicular

00:18:43.580 --> 00:18:44.957
that takes you to the top.

00:18:45.499 --> 00:18:45.958
Yeah.

00:18:45.958 --> 00:18:47.960
Okay.
Let's go and organize that.

00:18:50.504 --> 00:18:52.005
To take us up the funicular,

00:18:52.005 --> 00:18:55.092
we grabbed Harry,
Pathfinder 3 program manager

00:18:55.425 --> 00:18:56.885
and Ohio local.

00:18:59.596 --> 00:19:01.056
This is the part
where I'm supposed to tell you

00:19:01.056 --> 00:19:03.142
that it hasn't been certified
in 30 years.

00:19:03.392 --> 00:19:05.394
Hundred year old equipment... no.

00:19:06.520 --> 00:19:07.855
Okay, so everybody good?

00:19:07.855 --> 00:19:08.730
Braced, ready?

00:19:08.730 --> 00:19:09.857
I’m braced.

00:19:17.531 --> 00:19:19.992
Up goes the 90-year-old
elevator.

00:19:21.285 --> 00:19:24.121
Have I mentioned I’m
not a big fan of heights?

00:19:27.875 --> 00:19:29.084
And one more.

00:19:33.338 --> 00:19:33.964
Okay!

00:19:34.006 --> 00:19:35.549
Okay.

00:19:37.718 --> 00:19:39.928
You know, when you look up,
it looks kind of big, right?

00:19:40.095 --> 00:19:41.638
But then whenever
you get here, you look down

00:19:41.638 --> 00:19:43.098
and you see, like, trucks
and those gigantic-

00:19:43.098 --> 00:19:44.474
Holy crap.

00:19:44.474 --> 00:19:45.267
It looks like ants.

00:19:45.267 --> 00:19:46.685
The truck looks like nothing.

00:19:46.685 --> 00:19:48.770
Yeah, like a toy.

00:19:48.770 --> 00:19:50.314
Wow, that is crazy.

00:19:51.773 --> 00:19:54.234
This is making me feel
a little queasy.

00:20:00.532 --> 00:20:03.076
When you work with people here
or you grow up around here,

00:20:03.076 --> 00:20:05.120
everybody's got a story
about the airdock,

00:20:05.120 --> 00:20:06.121
whether you had
great grandparents

00:20:06.121 --> 00:20:07.706
or somebody
had worked here in the past,

00:20:07.706 --> 00:20:09.625
or you have a lot of family
that worked for Goodyear.

00:20:09.625 --> 00:20:11.376
Like I had grandparents
that worked at Goodyear.

00:20:14.880 --> 00:20:16.215
But seeing the blimp overhead,

00:20:16.215 --> 00:20:17.341
it didn't really mean
much as a kid.

00:20:17.341 --> 00:20:18.258
It was a beautiful thing
and it's

00:20:18.258 --> 00:20:19.301
such a cool thing to see it.

00:20:19.301 --> 00:20:20.802
But then you realize, well,
this is really special

00:20:20.802 --> 00:20:21.762
for, for us, right?

00:20:21.762 --> 00:20:22.387
Because, you know,

00:20:22.387 --> 00:20:24.431
you go a lot of places,
you don't really see this.

00:20:24.431 --> 00:20:25.766
And so you just have this kind

00:20:25.766 --> 00:20:28.101
of emotional attachment to it
every time you see it.

00:20:33.482 --> 00:20:34.316
It almost feels like

00:20:34.316 --> 00:20:36.902
this place has been waiting
patiently for decades

00:20:36.902 --> 00:20:39.488
for airship building
to start up again.

00:20:43.450 --> 00:20:46.870
And that's a lovely notion.
But there's every reason

00:20:46.870 --> 00:20:50.082
to think
all of this might fail.

00:20:50.082 --> 00:20:52.626
Sergey, in fact, has invested
in airships before.

00:20:53.961 --> 00:20:56.046
He backed a company called
Airship Ventures

00:20:56.046 --> 00:21:00.217
that flew people on joy rides
around the Bay Area.

00:21:00.217 --> 00:21:02.594
But it folded
after a couple of years.

00:21:04.137 --> 00:21:06.014
Long range trips? Hauling

00:21:06.014 --> 00:21:08.225
hundreds of tons of cargo?

00:21:08.225 --> 00:21:09.977
Even dicier.

00:21:09.977 --> 00:21:11.228
So what do you make of

00:21:11.228 --> 00:21:13.272
LTA and Sergey?

00:21:13.272 --> 00:21:15.023
You've got this guy
who's got enough money

00:21:15.023 --> 00:21:18.902
to do whatever he wants
and he's not even immediately

00:21:18.902 --> 00:21:21.280
going after tourism,
is going more after,

00:21:21.280 --> 00:21:23.615
he says, disaster
relief and cargo.

00:21:23.615 --> 00:21:25.701
I mean, is it
exciting to see somebody

00:21:25.701 --> 00:21:28.537
trying to build
an new large ship?

00:21:28.537 --> 00:21:29.413
It must be for you

00:21:29.413 --> 00:21:31.665
to have somebody
putting money into this.

00:21:31.665 --> 00:21:33.166
How honest do you
want me to be?

00:21:33.166 --> 00:21:34.001
Very honest.

00:21:34.001 --> 00:21:34.751
Okay.

00:21:35.168 --> 00:21:38.797
I don't want to piss off
any of these companies

00:21:38.797 --> 00:21:41.091
and kudos to them for
giving it a go.

00:21:41.091 --> 00:21:43.802
But to this day,
I find it difficult

00:21:43.802 --> 00:21:47.347
to imagine the cargo.

00:21:47.347 --> 00:21:47.806
Yeah.

00:21:47.806 --> 00:21:48.473
Disaster relief.

00:21:48.473 --> 00:21:49.975
I find that difficult.

00:21:49.975 --> 00:21:51.935
I think that's going to take
a very long time.

00:21:51.935 --> 00:21:53.270
I'm not saying
it's impossible,

00:21:53.270 --> 00:21:55.397
but it's going to take
a very long time to iron

00:21:55.397 --> 00:21:58.358
out the issues
with something that big.

00:21:59.609 --> 00:22:00.777
Al Weston, though,

00:22:00.777 --> 00:22:02.654
remains a true believer.

00:22:02.654 --> 00:22:03.947
I'm curious
where you see all this going.

00:22:03.947 --> 00:22:05.365
I know when you give your

00:22:05.365 --> 00:22:08.452
history of airships,
you start by saying, I'm

00:22:08.452 --> 00:22:09.578
Al Weston, I’m here

00:22:09.578 --> 00:22:11.830
to darken the skies,
which would imply

00:22:11.830 --> 00:22:13.707
that you want to make
a lot of airships, you know?

00:22:13.707 --> 00:22:17.002
Is that the plan
or is this, is this, you know,

00:22:17.002 --> 00:22:20.213
a limited number of vehicles
for the disaster relief

00:22:20.213 --> 00:22:20.881
type efforts?

00:22:20.881 --> 00:22:23.008
What are you picturing?

00:22:23.008 --> 00:22:24.176
From the beginning of this

00:22:24.176 --> 00:22:26.470
I've been really intrigued

00:22:26.470 --> 00:22:28.347
about the question,

00:22:28.347 --> 00:22:30.849
can these airships be made quickly

00:22:30.849 --> 00:22:33.560
and affordably enough to be
mass produced?

00:22:33.727 --> 00:22:35.812
Is, does it make sense to make

00:22:35.812 --> 00:22:37.564
100 or a thousand?

00:22:41.026 --> 00:22:43.070
Studies have suggested that

00:22:43.070 --> 00:22:45.364
there is a market,
but can we actually do it?

00:22:46.365 --> 00:22:49.117
I think that we’re standing
on the shoulders of giants,

00:22:49.117 --> 00:22:50.911
but at the same time,
we're leveraging

00:22:50.911 --> 00:22:53.914
new technologies,
new software, new materials

00:22:53.914 --> 00:22:56.917
that the people in the past
could only have dreamt of.

00:22:56.917 --> 00:23:01.004
I believe that
we can make a lot of airships,

00:23:01.421 --> 00:23:02.839
but we have to complete
the first one.

00:23:02.839 --> 00:23:03.715
Right.

00:23:05.384 --> 00:23:07.928
There are plenty of reasons
why airships aren't practical.

00:23:08.929 --> 00:23:10.055
They're slow,

00:23:10.514 --> 00:23:12.224
they're complicated,

00:23:12.224 --> 00:23:13.934
they're expensive.

00:23:13.934 --> 00:23:16.228
They can only fly in specific
weather conditions.

00:23:16.645 --> 00:23:19.564
The list goes on.

00:23:19.606 --> 00:23:21.525
But there's a feeling
that you get when you see

00:23:21.525 --> 00:23:24.945
these massive sky whales
drifting through the clouds.

00:23:24.945 --> 00:23:27.322
It's a feeling of wonder
and of whimsy.

00:23:28.031 --> 00:23:30.575
And I think it's that feeling
that keeps the dreams

00:23:30.575 --> 00:23:32.619
of airships aloft.

